  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold front pushing into the region late today \nwill bring another round of scattered showers and thunderstorms.\n\nA cold front has started to develop showers and thunderstorms\nacross the Ohio valley this afternoon. These showers and\nthunderstorms are expected to translate east through the\nremainder of the afternoon and evening today, and arrive across\nportions of SE West Virginia after sunset. These storms are\nexpected to dissipate as they track through the higher terrain\nlocations of eastern West Virginia; however, they may still\nproduce strong to severe winds before hitting the VA/WV state\nline where most CAM guidance has these storms dissipating. \n\nDuring the mid afternoon hours, CAM guidance has showers and\nthunderstorms developing along the Blue Ridge and pushing east\ninto the Piedmonts of VA/NC. Currently a low stratus deck has\nlingered across these areas all morning and this afternoon;\nhowever, it is slowly starting to dissipate. Again, these storms\nwill likely remain scattered in nature for the area as they\ntranslate east through the remainder of the afternoon and into\nthe evening. The largest threat from these storms, and the\nstorms aforementioned above for the late evening across SE West\nVirginia, will be the potential for damaging wind gusts that \nmay blow down trees and power poles leading to localized power \noutages. This threat primarily stems from DCAPE values in the \n800-1000 J/Kg range across the area thanks to dry air aloft and \nat the surface. SBCAPE values look to remain modest at around \n1000-2000 J/Kg across the region, and deep layer shear will be \nsufficient enough in the 25-30 knot range to produce \nmulticellular clusters from storms that do develop today. \n\nBehind the frontal passage during the overnight hours, winds\nwill transition to northwesterly as surface high pressure builds\ninto the region. This will bring gusty winds during the\novernight hours to the area, which will linger through the mid\nmorning hours on Sunday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Drier cooler weather returns Sunday and continues\ninto early next week.\n\nBehind the frontal passage on Sunday, surface high pressure\nejecting southeastward out of Canada will inject cooler and\ndrier air into the region on Sunday. This will lead to initially\ncloudy conditions across upslope regions in the mountains, but\nshould quickly clear out by the mid morning and early afternoon\nhours on Sunday. Mostly clear skies with no rain chances are\nexpected through Tuesday, with isolated to scattered chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms returning to the area by the middle of\nnext week. More moisture looks to return to the area by Thursday\nas the high pressure over the region finally breaks down\nallowing the Bermuda high to take over, which will advect more\nmoisture to the area.",
